  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery reveals a gradual erosion of the \nlow stratus deck that lingered across much of the area through \nthe morning hours. Extensive morning cloud cover severely \nhindered diurnal warming, keeping temperatures unseasonably cool\nwith much of the region remaining in the 40s this afternoon.\n\nWith precipitation having fully exited eastern Idaho, the \nprimary short-term operational concern shifts to nighttime \nboundary-layer evolution. While confidence in complete boundary-\nlayer decoupling remains moderate, abundant residual low-level \nmoisture makes the redevelopment of low stratus and patchy fog \nprobable overnight into Tuesday morning. Areas that see \nsustained clearing around sunset will have the highest \nlikelihood of seeing fog formation before boundary-layer mixing \nresumes Tuesday afternoon.\n\nA tranquil and predominantly dry pattern will dominate the \nremainder of the workweek. Operational guidance continues to \ntrack a series of weak upper-level impulses crossing the region \nmid-to-late week late tomorrow and again Thursday PM into Friday\nAM. These disturbances will periodically increase cloud cover \nand could generate isolated, low-end precipitation chances over \nthe mountains, but dynamic lift is weak and no organized, \nimpactful storm systems are expected. Temperature profiles will \nremain largely stagnant day-to-day through Friday, with \nafternoon highs continuing near or slightly below late-autumn \nclimatology.\n\nMedium-range model guidance remains in strong consensus\nregarding a pronounced pattern change heading into the upcoming\nweekend. Ensembles are steadfast in building and amplifying an\nupper-level ridge directly over the Intermountain West. Strong\nsubsidence beneath the ridge will maintain completely dry\nconditions across eastern Idaho, while rising 500 mb heights\ninitiate a steady warming trend. Daytime maximum temperatures\nwill rebound to above-normal levels starting late this weekend\nand likely holding into early next week.",
